sections:
  - eyebrow: "The problem"
    h: "What the tenant is exposed to at signing"
    html: "When a company takes on a leased unit, it inherits a property whose real condition rarely matches what the contract describes. The risk does not surface on signing day but at lease end: when the keys are handed back, the landlord may pursue a <b>dilapidations</b> claim for repairs to damage that —in many cases— already existed before entry. Without dated technical evidence, that dispute comes down to each party's word.<br><br>A pre-lease schedule of condition reverses that logic: it records the state of the property <b>before</b> signing, with photographic and technical support, so that at lease end there is an objective reference for which damage is attributable to occupation and which pre-existed."
  - eyebrow: "The instruction"
    h: "Methodology: visual inspection and functional testing"
    html: "The instruction, issued by the incoming tenant in January 2026, was carried out through a <b>visual technical inspection</b> of the whole unit —928 m² at ground floor and 392 m² of first-floor offices— and <b>functional testing</b> of the building services. This is not a photo report: each element is assessed, with the judgement of a <a href=\"/en/construction-expert/\">construction expert</a>, against its governing regulation and its maintenance status, distinguishing a genuine defect from acceptable wear."
  - eyebrow: "The findings"
    h: "Grading severity: functional versus cosmetic"
    html: "The inspection recorded pre-existing defects of very different severity. The value of the report lies not in listing damage but in <b>grading</b> it: separating what compromises safety or weathertightness from what is purely cosmetic.<ul style=\"margin:18px 0 0; padding-left:20px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;\"><li><b>Emergency lighting without reliable operation.</b> Self-contained units that failed to switch on correctly or lacked sufficient autonomy: a direct breach of the required means-of-escape conditions.</li><li><b>Fire-protection equipment out of maintenance.</b> Hose reels (BIEs) and extinguishers without a current annual service record. Without that certification, the operability of the fire-fighting means is not guaranteed under RIPCI.</li><li><b>Deck-type roof with active water ingress.</b> Staining, tide marks and efflorescence on the suspended ceiling, with areas partly dismantled by earlier, unfinished repairs. This is the item with the greatest cost exposure in the file.</li><li><b>Degraded weather seals to the office glazing.</b> Loss of insulation and a near-term risk of water ingress.</li><li><b>A cracked façade cladding panel — cosmetic only.</b> Here the report does the opposite of the points above: it limits scope. The crack compromises neither the fixing nor the weathertightness of the panel, so it should carry no future repair liability. Scoping a defect down matters as much as flagging a serious one.</li></ul>"
  - eyebrow: "The outcome"
    h: "Responsibilities allocated before signing"
    html: "The report fixes the condition of the property at a certain date and, on that basis, allocates maintenance responsibilities between landlord and tenant before signing. The fire-protection and emergency-lighting shortfalls are documented as pre-existing —not attributable to the incoming tenant— and the roof water ingress is flagged as an open item to resolve before entry. When the keys are handed back, neither party starts from zero: there is a technical photograph of the point of origin."
  - eyebrow: "Who it serves"
    h: "A preventive, recurring instrument"
    html: "This report is a preventive tool for <b>companies about to lease or hand back logistics and industrial units</b>, <b>industrial asset managers</b> and the <b>advisers</b> supporting the transaction. Its cost is a fraction of a dilapidations dispute at lease end, and its value grows when it becomes the standard protocol for the entry and exit of every property.<br><br>The same logic applies to the <b>periodic certification of a unit's condition</b> across its service life, beyond the moment of the lease."
